.Sh BUFFERS
.Nm
supports up to 20 open buffers.
Each buffer has its own undo history, dirty flag, and read-only state.
Special system buffers (the buffer list with
.Ic C-x C-b ,
the help buffer with
.Ic C-h )
have names enclosed in
.Sq *
and can be closed with
.Ic q
when another buffer is available to switch to.
.Pp
The kill ring is global and shared across all buffers, so text killed in
one buffer can be yanked in another.
It holds a single entry: a kill replaces what was there, consecutive
.Ic C-k
kills append to it, and there is no
.Ic M-y
yank-pop with which to reach an older one.
.Sh COMPILATION
.Ic M-x compile
prompts for a command line, prefilled with the current buffer's
.Va compile-command
.Pq default Ql make -k ,
and runs it with
.Ic /bin/sh -c
in the directory containing the source file.
For unsaved or special buffers the working directory is the current
directory
.Pq Fn getcwd .
.Pp
The confirmed command is stored back into the buffer's
.Va compile-command
so
.Ic M-x recompile
can repeat it without prompting.
An empty confirmed command reports
.Do
No compile command
.Dc .
.Pp
.Ic M-x recompile
re-runs the last compile command.
From an ordinary buffer it uses that buffer's
.Va compile-command ;
from
.Pa *compilation*
it reuses the last executed command and working directory.
.Pp
Output is captured asynchronously and streams incrementally into the
.Pa *compilation*
buffer, with combined stdout and stderr.
While compilation is running, editing and window management remain responsive.
The active compilation process can be terminated with
.Ic M-x kill-compilation
or
.Ic C-c C-k
inside the
.Pa *compilation*
buffer.
Retained output is capped at 8\~MiB.
Every kept byte counts against that cap, line terminators and the still
incomplete last line included, so neither a flood of blank lines nor one
endless line can grow the buffer past it.
Truncation is noted in the buffer; the process keeps running, its remaining
output is still read and discarded, and its exit status is still reported.
.Pp
Compilation is permitted while the source buffer is read-only.
.Sh READ-ONLY MODE
.Ic M-x read-only-mode
.Pq bound to Ic C-x C-q
toggles the current buffer between read-only and writable.
.Ic M-x toggle-read-only
is a compatibility alias.
.Pp
The read-only state is buffer-local and survives buffer and window
switches.
A read-only buffer rejects editing keys and mutating
.Ic M-x
commands, showing
.Do
Buffer is read-only
.Dc
in the status area.
.Pp
Opening a file with
.Fl R
or
.Ic C-x C-r
initially opens it read-only;
.Ic C-x C-q
can subsequently make such a buffer writable.
.Pp
The mode line shows an
.Do
RO
.Dc
indicator when the buffer is read-only.
.Sh LOCAL VARIABLES
.Nm
supports a limited subset of Emacs file-local variables, parsed
without evaluation.
Two variables are recognized:
.Bl -tag -width "compile-command"
A numeric argument zaps through that many occurrences.
.It Va compile-command
A quoted string such as
.Ql "make all && ./a.out" .
.It Va buffer-read-only
Either
.Ql t
or
.Ql nil .
.El
.Pp
Variables are set through two mechanisms:
.Bl -enum
.It
First-line
.Fl *- ... No -*- Sy modeline
.Pq or second-line after a Li #! shebang line .
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* -*- compile-command: "make all && ./a.out"; -*- */
.Ed
.It
End-of-file
.Sy "Local Variables:"
\&...
.Sy End:
block, searched within the final 3000 bytes of the file
.Po honoring a final form feed Pc .
The comment prefix and suffix are derived from the
.Sy "Local Variables:"
line.
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Local Variables:
# compile-command: "make test"
# buffer-read-only: t
# End:
.Ed
.El
.Pp
String values may span multiple lines with backslash-newline continuation:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Local Variables:
# compile-command: "cc foo.c \e
#   -o foo"
# End:
.Ed
.Pp
Precedence: footer overrides modeline; file-local overrides
directory-local.
.Pp
.Ar eval
is ignored; unknown variables are ignored; merely visiting a file
never runs
.Va compile-command .
Execution happens only through an explicit
.Ic M-x compile
or
.Ic M-x recompile .
.Sh DIRECTORY-LOCAL VARIABLES
.Nm
implements a safe, deliberately limited subset of
.Pa .dir-locals.el .
A file named
.Pa .dir-locals.el
is searched for upward from the visited file's directory to the
filesystem root; the nearest match wins.
.Pp
Only the generic
.Ql nil
selector is applied.
Supported variables are
.Va compile-command
and
.Va buffer-read-only ;
all other entries
.Pq including Ql eval , Ql mode , Ql subdirs ,
.Ql auto-mode-alist , mode-specific selectors, vectors, and backquote
are ignored without evaluation.
The optional leading
.Sq '
quote is accepted.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
((nil . ((compile-command . "make all")
         (buffer-read-only . t))))
.Ed
.Pp
Defensive limits: 64\~KiB file, 64-deep nesting, 4096 tokens.
File-local variables override directory-local.
.Pp
.Em No Emacs Lisp is evaluated
from
.Pa .dir-locals.el .
.Sh EXTERNAL CHANGES
.Nm
records each open file's device, inode, size and nanosecond timestamps as
last seen.
A background poll catches files that have been rewritten, replaced or
removed by another process, and tags the affected buffer with a
.Sq (changed)
marker in the mode line.
A file that cannot be examined at all — an unreadable directory, an I/O
error, a symlink loop — is tagged the same way: unable to check is never
reported as unchanged.
On
.Ic C-x C-s
against such a buffer the user is prompted to confirm before overwriting,
either
.Sq File ... changed on disk.  Save anyway?
or, when the file cannot be examined at all,
.Sq Cannot verify ... on disk.  Save anyway? .
Both default to no.
.Ic C-x s
asks the same question for each buffer it offers to save.
.Pp
.Ic C-x C-w
asks a different question about a different file: whenever the name typed
there already exists and is not the file the buffer already holds, it
prompts
.Sq File ... exists.  Overwrite?
before the buffer adopts the name.
Declining leaves the buffer, its name and its recorded file state exactly
as they were.
.Pp
A save writes to a temporary file beside the target, flushes it, checks one
last time that the target is still the file the user agreed to replace, and
only then renames it into place and flushes the directory entry.
If the target changed in between, nothing is written and the buffer reports
.Sq ... changed on disk during the save; nothing written .
Saving through a symbolic link replaces the file the link points at, not
the link.
.Pp
With
.Ic auto-revert-mode
(or its global counterpart
.Ic global-auto-revert-mode )
turned on, a clean buffer whose file has changed on disk is silently
reloaded so the view always matches the file.
A file that has been removed, or that cannot be examined, is only marked,
never reverted: the buffer is then the last copy of its contents.
Modified buffers are never reverted automatically — the
.Sq (changed)
marker stays visible until either the user saves (and is prompted) or
explicitly runs
.Ic M-x revert-buffer
to discard local edits.
.Sh WINDOWS
The terminal can be divided into multiple windows with
.Ic C-x 2
(horizontal split) or
.Ic C-x 3
(vertical split).
Each window shows one buffer and maintains its own scroll position and cursor.
Multiple windows may show the same buffer.
Use
.Ic C-x o
to cycle between windows.
Up to 8 windows may be open simultaneously.
.Pp
The smallest terminal
.Nm
lays out for is 3 rows by 8 columns: one text row, one mode line and the
echo area.
A smaller terminal is treated as that size, so the display overflows but
the editor keeps working; a reported size larger than 10000 in either
direction is rejected as implausible and the previous size is kept.
.Sh CONTROL CHARACTERS
Nothing
.Nm
reads becomes something the terminal obeys.
Bytes from a file, a filename, a directory listing, a Lisp string or a
subprocess' output are always drawn as characters, never as terminal
control sequences, whatever mode or syntax the buffer is in.
.Pp
A control character is shown in caret notation:
.Ql ^@
through
.Ql ^_
for the bytes below a space, and
.Ql ^?
for
.Ql DEL .
The C1 controls
.Pq U+0080 No through U+009F ,
which an eight-bit terminal reads as
.Ql CSI ,
.Ql OSC
and
.Ql DCS
introducers, and bytes that are not valid UTF-8 at all, are shown as
.Ql \e Ns Ar xnn
with the byte's value in lowercase hexadecimal, one spelling per byte.
.Pp
These spellings occupy the columns they are drawn in, so the cursor, the
mode line's column indicator, tab stops and rectangles all count a
.Ql ^[
as two columns and a
.Ql \ex9b
as four.
Tabs are not control characters here: they expand to the next tab stop as
usual.
.Sh SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING
Syntax highlighting is detected automatically from the file extension or
type, e.g., Makefile.  Hash-bang lines
.Pq #!
are used as a fallback when the extension is not recognised.
.Bl -column "JavaScript" ".js .mjs .cjs" -compact
.It Sy Language Ta Sy Extensions
.It C/C++ Ta .c .h .cpp .hpp .cc
.It Python Ta .py .pyw .pyi .pyx
.It Shell Ta .sh .bash .zsh .profile .bashrc .zshrc
.It JavaScript Ta .js .mjs .cjs
.It TypeScript Ta .ts .tsx .d.ts
.It Rust Ta .rs .rlib
.It Java Ta .java
.It C# Ta .cs .csx
.It PHP Ta .php .phtml
.It Ruby Ta .rb .rbw .rake
.It Swift Ta .swift
.It SQL Ta .sql .ddl .dml
.It Dart Ta .dart
.It HTML Ta .html .htm .xhtml
.It JSX Ta .jsx
.It Vue Ta .vue
