.El
.Ss Git Commit Mode
.Nm
recognizes
.Pa COMMIT_EDITMSG ,
.Pa MERGE_MSG ,
.Pa SQUASH_MSG ,
.Pa TAG_EDITMSG ,
.Pa NOTES_EDITMSG
and
.Pa EDIT_DESCRIPTION
by filename and switches into a dedicated mode, shown as
.Dq Git commit
in the mode line.
As with the rebase todo, the match is on the exact basename only, so
.Pa .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG
switches the mode while a file whose name merely contains the string
does not; the mode binds keys that quit the editor.
Lines starting with
.Sq #
are dimmed as comments, and characters past column 50 on the subject
line (the first non-blank, non-comment line) are shown in red as a
length warning.
.Pp
.Ic C-c C-c
saves the buffer and exits
.Nm
with status 0, letting git proceed with the commit.
.Ic C-c C-k
prompts for confirmation, then exits without saving and with a
non-zero status, so git cancels the commit.
These two bindings are built in and only active in commit buffers; they
take priority over any user Lisp binding for the same keys.
.Ic M-q
leaves the subject line and comment paragraphs untouched; body
paragraphs still reflow to column 72 as usual.
.Pp
.Ic C-x #
saves the current buffer and exits with status 0 in
.Em any
buffer, not just commit buffers.
It serves the broader
.Ev EDITOR Ns / Ns Ev GIT_EDITOR
server flow shared by
.Xr crontab 1 ,
.Xr sudoedit 8 ,
.Xr hg 1
and similar tools.
.Ss Git Rebase Mode
A file named exactly
.Pa git-rebase-todo
(the todo list of
.Ic git rebase -i ,
found at
.Pa .git/rebase-merge/git-rebase-todo )
switches into a dedicated mode, shown as
.Dq Git rebase
in the mode line.
The match is on the exact basename only, so files that merely contain
the string in their name are unaffected.
Action words
.Pq Ic pick , reword , edit , squash , fixup , drop , exec , break , label , reset , merge , update-ref
and their single-letter abbreviations are highlighted as keywords, the
commit hash after them as a constant, an
.Ic exec
command body as a string, and comment lines are dimmed.
An unrecognized action word is shown in warning color, since a typo
there makes git fail the whole rebase; so is an option flag the action
cannot take, since only
.Ic fixup
and
.Ic merge
accept
.Fl C
or
.Fl c .
.Pp
.Ic C-c C-p ,
.Ic C-c C-r ,
.Ic C-c C-e ,
.Ic C-c C-s ,
.Ic C-c C-f
and
.Ic C-c C-d
rewrite the action of the current line to
.Ic pick , reword , edit , squash , fixup
or
.Ic drop
respectively; they refuse to touch comment,
.Ic exec
and other non-commit lines.
Rewriting a
.Ic fixup
line that carries a
.Fl C
or
.Fl c
flag to any other action also deletes the flag, which only
.Ic fixup
accepts.
.Ic M-p
and
.Ic M-n
move the current line up and down, for reordering commits.
The same commands are available as
.Ic M-x git-rebase-pick , git-rebase-reword , git-rebase-edit ,
.Ic git-rebase-squash , git-rebase-fixup , git-rebase-drop ,
.Ic git-rebase-move-line-up
and
.Ic git-rebase-move-line-down .
.Pp
As in commit buffers,
.Ic C-c C-c
saves the todo list and exits with status 0 so the rebase proceeds, and
.Ic C-c C-k
prompts for confirmation, then exits without saving and with a non-zero
status, making git abort the rebase.
All these bindings are built in, only active in rebase buffers, and
take priority over user Lisp bindings for the same keys.
.Ss Dired Mode
.Ic M-x dired
and
.Ic C-x d
prompt for a directory and list it in a read-only buffer, shown as
.Dq Dired
in the mode line.
The prompt is the ordinary path picker, where
.Ic Enter
on a directory descends into it rather than submitting it.
There are three ways to name a directory rather than descend into it:
.Ic M-Enter
submits the typed text literally (a deliberate deviation from Emacs),
appending
.Sq /\&.
and pressing
.Ic Enter
does the same and is what Emacs itself does, and answering with
something inside the directory works too, since a non-directory answer
means the directory containing it.
An empty answer means the directory of the current buffer.
.Ic C-x C-f
on a directory lists it as well, reusing the listing buffer for that
directory if one is already open, and so does naming a directory on the
command line
.Pq Ic kg src .
.Pp
The listing buffer is named
.Pa *Dired: /abs/path* ,
and that name is the mode's only state: two directories listed at once
are simply two buffers.
Row 0 is a header naming the directory; every row after it is one entry
behind a two-column mark gutter, with directories suffixed
.Sq / .
Entries are sorted with
.Xr strcoll 3 ,
hidden files are listed, and
.Sq \&.
and
.Sq \&..
are omitted, the header line and
.Ic ^
covering them.
The header is dimmed, directory names are colored as keywords, and a
mark or flag in the gutter is colored where it stands.
.Pp
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It Enter Ta Visit the entry at point (dired-find-file)
.It ^ Ta List the parent directory (dired-up-directory)
.It g Ta Re-read the directory (dired-revert)
.It n / p Ta Move to the next / previous line
.It m Ta Mark the entry with * (dired-mark)
.It d Ta Flag the entry with D (dired-flag-file-deletion)
.It u Ta Clear the mark or flag (dired-unmark)
.It x Ta Delete the flagged entries (dired-do-flagged-delete)
.It q Ta Close the listing
.El
.Pp
.Ic Enter
on a directory lists it in place of the current listing; on anything
else it opens the entry exactly as
.Ic C-x C-f
would, so an entry removed behind
.Nm Ns 's
back reports the usual open error and
.Ic g
recovers.
A symbolic link to a directory is descended into; a broken one lists,
and opens, as a plain file.
.Ic m ,
.Ic d
and
.Ic u
write their marker into column 0 and step down one line, so a run of
entries takes one keystroke each, and
.Ic u
clears either marker.
The mark and the flag are distinct, as in Emacs:
.Ic x
acts on the
.Sq D
flags only, after a single
.Dq Delete N flagged entries? (y/n)
confirmation.
Each flagged entry's identity is recorded before that question is asked
and checked again at the deletion, against a descriptor held open on the
listed directory for the whole operation.
An entry that has since been removed, replaced by a different object, or
changed kind is skipped and counted, never deleted in the confirmed
entry's place.
Deletion is never recursive and never follows a symbolic link: a
directory is removed as a directory, so a non-empty one fails and says
so, a symbolic link is unlinked as the link it is even where the listing
marks it with a
.Sq /
because it points at a directory, and everything else is unlinked.
A failure does not stop the rest; the counts that failed and that were
skipped for having changed are reported, with the first error seen, and
the listing is re-read afterwards.
.Pp
Marks are the buffer's own text rather than editor state, so they take
no undo record, never make the buffer modified, and are dropped by
.Ic g
along with the listing that carried them.
In this version the
.Sq *
mark is inert: the commands that would consume it (copy, rename,
deleting the marked entries) are not implemented yet, and only the
.Sq D
flag has an action.
Copy, rename, mkdir and unmark-all are missing for the same reason,
along with
.Xr ls 1 Ns -style
size and permission columns.
.Pp
Every listing key is an
.Ic M-x
command as well:
.Ic M-x dired , dired-find-file , dired-up-directory , dired-revert ,
.Ic dired-mark , dired-flag-file-deletion , dired-unmark
and
.Ic dired-do-flagged-delete ;
all but the first refuse politely outside a listing.
The listing keys are built in rather than Lisp bindings, since user
bindings are
.Ic C-c
sequences by design, so nothing can shadow them and the mode is
identical in a
.Li WITH_LISP=0
build.
.Ss YAML Mode
Files ending in
.Pa .yaml
or
.Pa .yml
use YAML syntax highlighting automatically;
.Ic M-x yaml-mode
enables it manually for the current buffer, e.g. for an extensionless
file.
Highlighting is lexical and does not validate YAML: it colors mapping
keys, comments, quoted scalars, block scalars
.Pq Sq \&|
and
.Pq Sq >
(including across blank lines, tracking the block's indentation),
booleans/null, numbers, and structural markers such as
.Sq --- ,
.Sq ... ,
and
.Sq - ,
plus anchors, aliases and tags.
.Sh AUTOCOMPLETE
When electric pairing is enabled,
.Nm
automatically inserts matching closing brackets when an opening bracket is
typed, provided the cursor is at the end of a line.
Recognised pairs are
.Sq () ,
.Sq [] ,
and
.Sq {} .
Autocomplete is suppressed when text arrives quickly, such as during a paste
operation.
.Pp
Electric pairing is off by default.
Toggle it with
.Ic M-x electric-pair-mode ,
or enable it at startup by adding
.Ql (command-execute \(aqelectric-pair-mode)
to the Lisp init file (see
.Sx FILES ) .
.Sh GOTO LINE
.Ic M-g
prompts for a line number, optionally followed by a colon and a column
number
.Pq e.g. Ar 42:10 .
The cursor is moved to that position and the view is centred vertically.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Nm
uses the controlling terminal
.Pa /dev/tty
for raw-mode input and output.
Terminal size changes
.Pq SIGWINCH
are handled gracefully.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /dev/tty
Terminal device used for raw-mode I/O.
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kg/init.fe
Lisp init file, loaded at startup unless
.Fl Q
is given.
Falls back to
.Pa ~/.config/kg/init.fe .
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kg/lisp/
Package directory searched by
.Li (load "name") .
